  "_source": "countinghouse docs/superpowers/specs/shared-runtime-wire/vectors.json topLevelModules, at countinghouse 269ae6ac (#449, M5b: the top-level rule on own properties): copied, never edited here",
  "topLevelModules": {
    "rule": "a module is top-level when it is the scenario of a pack in packs: of that pack's modules (in their order), the first that modules gives role \"scenario\", else the pack's first module; and only when modules holds it. Every other module in modules is a part, whatever its role, and has no enabled.<module>. Only own properties are read (Object.hasOwn), so a name like \"constructor\" is a name like any other",
    "cases": [
      {
        "why": "a pack's scenario and its part",
        "record": {
          "modules": {
            "@countinghouse/ch-pack-example": {
              "version": "1.0.0",
              "role": "scenario"
            },
            "@countinghouse/ch-step-example": {
              "version": "0.2.0",
              "role": "part"
            }
          },
          "packs": {
            "example": {
              "modules": {
                "@countinghouse/ch-pack-example": "1.0.0",
                "@countinghouse/ch-step-example": "0.2.0"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "topLevel": [
          "@countinghouse/ch-pack-example"
        ]
      },
      {
